On Sun, Sep 22, 2013 at 9:47 AM, Jacob Errington <nictytan@gmail.com> wrote:

> On 22 September 2013 01:18, Robin Lee Powell <rlpowell@digitalkingdom.org>wrote:
>
>>
>> "I'm willing to take you to play with the puppies, but I'm not
>> willing to carry you".
>>
>>
> .i mi te mukti lo nu do se kansa co klama lo nu kelci lo gerku cifnu kei
> kei .enai lo nu bevri do
>
> My rationale is that when you're willing do to something, it's that there
> is some kind of motivation to do it, hence {mukti}.
>
> .i mi'e la tsani mu'o
>
I think this is where the phrase came from etymologically, and is still
related to what it actually means. However, I think there is also an
element of "It's not a problem for me to take you to play with the puppies,
but it is a problem for me to have to carry you." This form is easy to
translate: {nabmi fa ge lo nu mi kansa do lo nu klama lo nu kelci lo gerku
cifnu gi nai lo nu mi bevri do} (or a less fancy version without
forethought). sa'e I don't think this fully expresses the concept, nor do I
think your idea fully expresses the concept; I think both are involved, and
maybe other things as well.

mi'e la latro'a mu'o

